1
00:00:00,680 --> 00:00:05,520
and so on.

2
00:00:05,886.666666666667 --> 00:00:12,200
So let me continue.

3
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,780
On the RS feed thing, basically it's an

4
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:19,520
RS feed that constantly changes, right?

5
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:23,180
So depending on how people publish the application,

6
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:26,870
for example, if I pull from that RS feed today, I get the

7
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:30,380
latest five articles, but in one week, the latest five

8
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:34,240
articles are different, right? Because they're

9
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:38,300
always the latest. So like this, you can pull information

10
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:42,590
from all kinds of sources, very easily. But anyway,

11
00:00:42,590 --> 00:00:46,450
this is the input, basically a bunch of URLs,

12
00:00:46,450 --> 00:00:50,370
static or moving, depending on the type

13
00:00:50,370 --> 00:00:53,970
of thread. And then on the architectural side,

14
00:00:53,970 --> 00:00:58,620
this is what happens. So we have first an orchestrator

15
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:02,300
that gets as input all these URLs. And

16
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:06,220
depending on the platform, like Substack,

17
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:09,730
YouTube, GitHub or custom URLs, it kind

18
00:01:09,730 --> 00:01:13,790
of flattens them out over here and

19
00:01:13,790 --> 00:01:17,590
groups them depending on the URL. And

20
00:01:17,590 --> 00:01:21,460
it creates multiple shards. Basically

21
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:25,440
it creates a big list of URLs and splits it into

22
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:29,560
multiple pieces, which are known as shards.

23
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:33,460
And then each piece, each shard is delegated

24
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:37,280
to a new worker, right? But how that is done

25
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:41,150
is that you take that shard and this process is known

26
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:44,970
as fan out, where basically you put

27
00:01:44,970 --> 00:01:48,500
each piece on a new worker. And because

28
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:52,190
maybe you don't have enough capacity between delegating

29
00:01:52,190 --> 00:01:56,060
that work, that piece of work, which in our

30
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:59,720
use case is a slice of URLs, before

31
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:03,570
actually starting to process it,

32
00:02:03,570 --> 00:02:07,420
you put it in a queue, right? So basically you take

33
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:11,430
this big, this huge chunk of URLs, which

34
00:02:11,430 --> 00:02:15,520
can be in the order of millions, right?

35
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:19,390
You chunk it up, you split it into multiple pieces

36
00:02:19,390 --> 00:02:23,180
where each piece is processed on a single worker.

37
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:26,900
But instead of directly starting to process

38
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:30,530
it, you put each piece as a future job to be

39
00:02:30,530 --> 00:02:34,590
done in a queue. And then you have a limited

40
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:38,120
number of workers that take each piece from

41
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:42,040
this queue and start to process it, right?

42
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:45,990
And for example, you can have three workers

43
00:02:45,990 --> 00:02:50,440
and you can have 100 slices to be processed

44
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:54,310
or you can have 10 workers and 1000 slices. So

45
00:02:54,310 --> 00:02:58,060
basically they're not one-on-one, but like this,

46
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:01,740
you can have like huge spikes in your ingestion

47
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:05,290
and through this queue, you can handle all

48
00:03:05,836.666666666657 --> 00:03:09,460
the spikes independent on how many workers you have.

49
00:03:09,460 --> 00:03:13,060
The only limitation is the speed on how you

50
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:16,770
process them. And if you want to go faster, you just add another

51
00:03:16,770 --> 00:03:20,930
worker into this pool and you process them faster. So basically

52
00:03:20,930 --> 00:03:24,670
this is how you take your initial input and delegate it into

53
00:03:24,670 --> 00:03:28,200
multiple processes, which are known as the workers.

54
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:32,070
And each worker will basically run in this use case, this data,

55
00:03:32,070 --> 00:03:35,850
this pipeline, which is pretty common

56
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:39,390
to agentic memory stuff,

57
00:03:40,426.66666666665697 --> 00:03:44,580
right? And what this does is that it takes this.

58
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:48,400
So the same data pipeline runs on each worker,

59
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:52,360
but with different URLs, but each worker gets

60
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:55,930
basically a bunch, a list of URLs that needs

61
00:03:55,930 --> 00:03:59,680
to process. And what it does, it further flattens them

62
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:03,310
out because for example, it can get as a single input,

63
00:04:03,686.666666666657 --> 00:04:07,480
a single URLs or RS feeds where an RS feed

64
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:11,310
can have on it, it's on its own a list

65
00:04:11,310 --> 00:04:15,140
of URLs. So basically it just flattens everything

66
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:18,840
into a single list where every list points to a direct

67
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:22,570
article, let's say. Then using,

68
00:04:22,570 --> 00:04:26,990
then what we need to do is basically to scrape

69
00:04:26,990 --> 00:04:30,680
all that information from the internet.

70
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:34,460
And to do that, because you at this point,

71
00:04:35,26.66666666668607 --> 00:04:38,610
right? You also have at this point a

72
00:04:38,610 --> 00:04:42,130
list of URLs. And here your limitation is basically IO

73
00:04:42,130 --> 00:04:47,180
operations on the internet, right? You need to basically

74
00:04:47,180 --> 00:04:51,660
crawl the internet to do network operations.

75
00:04:51,660 --> 00:04:55,470
And because of that, you can very easily paralyze

76
00:04:55,976.6666666666861 --> 00:04:59,660
this operation through batch async IO, right?

77
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:04,400
You extract through a threading parallelism,

78
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:07,930
which is very good for IO operations to

79
00:05:07,930 --> 00:05:12,000
like in parallel scrape a batch of links

80
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,940
from this window of initial URLs that

81
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:20,120
you need to process. For example,

82
00:05:20,120 --> 00:05:23,880
let's say that on this worker, we need to process

83
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:27,740
1000 links, right over here, then we flatten them

84
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:32,030
out if necessary, and then we don't necessarily

85
00:05:32,030 --> 00:05:36,010
want to start processing all 1000 at a single

86
00:05:36,010 --> 00:05:39,540
time. But let's say that we batch them in batches

87
00:05:39,926.6666666666861 --> 00:05:43,440
of 100, and then those 100 we can process

88
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:47,090
in parallel at a time. And this is basically the

89
00:05:47,090 --> 00:05:50,970
difference. So we have initially one million documents,

90
00:05:50,970 --> 00:05:54,760
we split them into multiple workers. Let's say each

91
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:58,840
worker gets 1000 URLs, and then further

92
00:05:58,840 --> 00:06:02,660
in each worker, we can further split them into

93
00:06:03,236.66666666668607 --> 00:06:06,930
batches and process them like, let's say 100

94
00:06:06,930 --> 00:06:11,200
by 100. And then we can do through

95
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:15,070
async IO or process in parallel each

96
00:06:15,070 --> 00:06:18,890
batch. That's basically what happens over here. And then

97
00:06:18,890 --> 00:06:22,480
we can also like transform them like, I don't know, clean, for example, the

98
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:26,640
retrieve HTML if necessary, and then using a similar strategy,

99
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:30,280
we load everything as documents into our memory,

100
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:33,930
which is as well IO bounded operations,

101
00:06:33,930 --> 00:06:37,560
because we need to take that document and just put

102
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:41,960
it in our database. So basically here the data

103
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:46,170
pipeline is constrained only by IO operations, network

104
00:06:46,170 --> 00:06:49,870
operations, and disk operations when it comes to the

105
00:06:49,870 --> 00:06:53,460
database. So basically now we have documents into the

106
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:57,370
unified agent memory. And now our goal is actually to take these documents

107
00:06:57,370 --> 00:07:01,020
and transform them into knowledge graph objects, which is it

108
00:07:01,020 --> 00:07:05,100
has a similar scanning strategy, but with different problems

109
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:08,660
because we need to use LLMs to process deformation and

110
00:07:08,660 --> 00:07:12,230
embedding models to embed it. So let's

111
00:07:12,230 --> 00:07:16,170
see how we do that. Okay,

112
00:07:16,170 --> 00:07:19,710
so basically now as input into this memory

113
00:07:19,710 --> 00:07:23,560
work pool, we get documents basically.

114
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:27,370
Let's say we have the same million, one million

115
00:07:27,370 --> 00:07:31,100
documents that we need to process. Let's stick

116
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:34,630
to this number, right? So at the beginning from

117
00:07:34,630 --> 00:07:38,740
architectural point of view, we do the same thing.

118
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:42,770
We have an orchestrator which just shards

119
00:07:42,770 --> 00:07:46,980
all these, which shards these one million

120
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:51,850
documents into slice of let's say 1000

121
00:07:51,850 --> 00:07:55,400
documents and fans all of this to the workers where

122
00:07:55,400 --> 00:07:58,970
first it puts each slice, each shard

123
00:07:58,970 --> 00:08:02,580
into the queue to be processed. And

124
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:06,430
then we have a pool of workers that take that

125
00:08:06,430 --> 00:08:10,070
slice to actually process it. But now we have the

126
00:08:10,070 --> 00:08:13,980
memory pipeline inside those workers that actually process

127
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:17,610
this information. And before going into the memory

128
00:08:17,610 --> 00:08:21,680
pipeline, I want to highlight that all these orchestrations,

129
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:25,650
sharding, queuing, worker, all this

130
00:08:25,650 --> 00:08:29,300
logic is done via prefact, right?

131
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:32,940
So prefact is the orchestrator tool that allows us to

132
00:08:32,940 --> 00:08:36,540
also do the data pipelines, which weren't there

133
00:08:36,540 --> 00:08:40,210
very good at, but they're also very good to

134
00:08:40,210 --> 00:08:43,820
actually do like rag ingestion or memory pipelines,

135
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:47,440
basically to do rag or for agent memory or for AI

136
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:50,980
stuff where we need to move data around,

137
00:08:50,980 --> 00:08:54,970
process it and load it into various storage

138
00:08:54,970 --> 00:08:59,130
units. It's basically the same problem as data engineering,

139
00:08:59,130 --> 00:09:03,800
but with a bit more complexity of it. Okay.

140
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:07,350
So now let's see what happens inside the memory

141
00:09:07,350 --> 00:09:11,430
pipeline. So as I said, we have documents as input,

142
00:09:11,430 --> 00:09:15,000
but here within a memory pipeline, we have only

143
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:19,640
a slice of the initial documents that we need to process,

144
00:09:19,640 --> 00:09:23,600
right? And we go through the standard steps,

145
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:27,840
like we first chunk the content of the

146
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:31,570
documents and then we further flatten all the

147
00:09:31,570 --> 00:09:35,290
chunks, right? Basically we have, let's say 1000 documents

148
00:09:35,290 --> 00:09:39,510
and after we chunk them, we can have like 10,000

149
00:09:40,396.66666666662786 --> 00:09:44,250
chunks, right? So now the numbers of units that

150
00:09:44,250 --> 00:09:47,870
we need to process grows depending on how long

151
00:09:47,870 --> 00:09:51,790
each document is, but let's say we have 10,000

152
00:09:51,790 --> 00:09:55,940
chunks here and that's why we further need to flatten them

153
00:09:55,940 --> 00:10:00,160
because it's a lot easier

154
00:10:00,706.6666666666279 --> 00:10:04,390
to process a flat list. And now

155
00:10:04,390 --> 00:10:08,310
the problem is that we don't have only a pure IO

156
00:10:08,310 --> 00:10:11,990
problem, right? We need to actually, what we need

157
00:10:11,990 --> 00:10:15,510
here is to extract knowledge graph triplets out

158
00:10:15,510 --> 00:10:19,110
of these chunks. Basically we need to extract those

159
00:10:19,110 --> 00:10:23,080
entity relationship, entity triplets for the content

160
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:26,670
of all those chunks. So basically we need an LLM to

161
00:10:26,670 --> 00:10:30,710
process this information. And a very common thing

162
00:10:30,710 --> 00:10:34,350
to do is here is to actually, well,

163
00:10:34,350 --> 00:10:38,310
use an API such as Gemini or Claude or whatever

164
00:10:38,310 --> 00:10:42,040
to basically have access to this LLM or

165
00:10:42,386.66666666662786 --> 00:10:46,460
take an open source LLM such as one from Liquid

166
00:10:46,460 --> 00:10:50,340
or QAN and host it on the VLLM server where

167
00:10:50,340 --> 00:10:54,290
we have GPUs, right? These are

168
00:10:54,290 --> 00:10:58,140
two common options. But

169
00:10:58,140 --> 00:11:01,700
how we hook the LLM to the memory pipeline is

170
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:05,460
actually quite similar regardless of

171
00:11:05,460 --> 00:11:09,370
how we host the LLM. And what we do is we

172
00:11:09,370 --> 00:11:13,800
want to use batch operations, right? As I said,

173
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:17,310
similar to the IO use case, we want to batch, for example,

174
00:11:17,310 --> 00:11:20,830
from these 10,000 units of

175
00:11:20,830 --> 00:11:24,710
chunks, we take 100 of them and we send all those

176
00:11:24,710 --> 00:11:29,240
100 to the LLM service to process at once.

177
00:11:29,240 --> 00:11:33,120
And in reality is also an IO problem.

178
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:36,700
So because the actual work is done on the LLM

179
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:40,840
server that has GPUs, that that's where the actual computation

180
00:11:40,840 --> 00:11:44,670
is done. Here in the memory pipeline, it can stay light and it just delegates

181
00:11:44,670 --> 00:11:48,840
that information to another server. Okay.

182
00:11:48,840 --> 00:11:52,380
Then we can do some, we have to do

183
00:11:52,380 --> 00:11:56,190
the normalization step where we

184
00:11:56,190 --> 00:11:59,980
do like, we do canonical name resolution and entity

185
00:11:59,980 --> 00:12:04,620
deduplication and that type of things. And here usually

186
00:12:04,620 --> 00:12:08,390
this is again very, uh, I, disk IO

187
00:12:08,390 --> 00:12:12,000
bounded because here we need to query

188
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:15,720
the database to actually find similar or

189
00:12:15,720 --> 00:12:19,350
similar entities. Right. So basically here we

190
00:12:19,350 --> 00:12:23,370
depend on the database. So it's again,

191
00:12:23,370 --> 00:12:27,280
a disk IO bounded. So again, we

192
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:30,840
can very easily paralyze it like that. And

193
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:34,930
another step is when we need to take the

194
00:12:34,930 --> 00:12:38,560
content of each entity and embed it.

195
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:42,310
And this can be done in a similar fashion to how we, we did it with

196
00:12:42,310 --> 00:12:46,100
the LLM, but because it's still a deep learning

197
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:50,080
model, but we can also take another spin here as an example

198
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:53,720
where we can host actually the embedding on the worker

199
00:12:53,720 --> 00:12:57,540
itself. Right. Because the embedding models are lighter

200
00:12:57,540 --> 00:13:01,250
and which means that we can actually run them on the CPU on the worker

201
00:13:01,250 --> 00:13:04,810
itself. So we stay local, which is faster

202
00:13:04,810 --> 00:13:08,480
because now we don't have like network IO

203
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:12,010
limitations at all because we are on the same

204
00:13:12,010 --> 00:13:15,680
machine and we can just run again batch

205
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:19,310
operations directly on that worker. And

206
00:13:19,310 --> 00:13:23,290
now as outputs, we have those knowledge

207
00:13:23,290 --> 00:13:27,020
graph objects, which we load in the

208
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:30,800
database, which probably it's again,

209
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:34,970
we have this loading step, which we again

210
00:13:34,970 --> 00:13:39,420
can batch and it's a disk and network

211
00:13:39,420 --> 00:13:43,190
IO bounded. So yeah, basically

212
00:13:43,190 --> 00:13:48,460
just to repeat, we have two levels of parallel

213
00:13:48,460 --> 00:13:52,180
list here that are very important,

214
00:13:52,546.6666666666279 --> 00:13:56,170
right? We have the, for example, for the, for the memory work

215
00:13:56,170 --> 00:13:59,810
pool, we have this list of documents, 1 million documents

216
00:13:59,810 --> 00:14:03,560
that we need to process. First what we do, we take that list

217
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:07,240
and shard it into slices and delegate that to workers

218
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:11,310
and each worker is basically a separate process or a separate

219
00:14:11,310 --> 00:14:15,110
machine altogether. And each worker gets

220
00:14:15,110 --> 00:14:20,290
like a slice, let's say 1 million, 1,000

221
00:14:20,290 --> 00:14:24,440
documents and further processes it and it processes

222
00:14:24,440 --> 00:14:28,320
it in parallel again by batching.

223
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:32,600
And that batching usually is IO bounded,

224
00:14:33,306.66666666662786 --> 00:14:37,000
right? Which means you can very easily run

225
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,540
in parallel multiple operations

226
00:14:40,540 --> 00:14:44,160
and like this, you can further as much as possible,

227
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:48,330
you can process in parallel all the elements

228
00:14:48,330 --> 00:14:52,180
that are sent to particular worker. But because

229
00:14:52,180 --> 00:14:55,840
in reality that's not really feasible, you batch it

230
00:14:55,840 --> 00:14:59,430
again into multiple slices and you process that

231
00:14:59,430 --> 00:15:03,120
in parallel. So we have two levels of slicing, the initial

232
00:15:03,120 --> 00:15:06,860
level of slicing where we take it and split it into workers

233
00:15:06,860 --> 00:15:10,500
and the second level of slicing where we split it

234
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:14,350
into batches. And using this architecture, depending

235
00:15:14,350 --> 00:15:17,970
on your workload, you can even go crazier and here we

236
00:15:17,970 --> 00:15:21,490
have like the, when we split the elements in

237
00:15:21,490 --> 00:15:26,270
multiple workers, that's like pipeline parallelism

238
00:15:26,270 --> 00:15:30,210
and when we further split it and parallelize it within

239
00:15:30,210 --> 00:15:36,960
the pipeline, that's task parallelizing.

240
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:40,670
And at the task parallelizing level, we can

241
00:15:40,670 --> 00:15:44,200
even go crazier and use like a desk or rate

242
00:15:44,200 --> 00:15:48,820
cluster and actually instead of processing

243
00:15:48,820 --> 00:15:52,780
it through VLM servers on the worker or

244
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:56,520
for example if we have like very hardcore

245
00:15:56,520 --> 00:16:00,160
embedding type of things to do and we don't want to

246
00:16:00,180 --> 00:16:04,580
run an embedding model on a VLM server, we can

247
00:16:04,580 --> 00:16:08,230
further take all of that and run it on the rate cluster

248
00:16:08,230 --> 00:16:12,320
or desk cluster to actually parallelize it even further

249
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:16,390
and run the whole batch at once in parallel

250
00:16:16,746.6666666666279 --> 00:16:20,640
for real on another cluster of GPUs.

251
00:16:20,640 --> 00:16:24,480
So you can get creative here as long as you properly

252
00:16:24,480 --> 00:16:28,360
